# Sealed Secrets

The first component Flux deploys here, and it is deliberately the smallest
useful one: it proves reconciliation end to end, and everything the secrets
model does later depends on it existing.

## Provenance

`controller.yaml` is the vendor's release asset, unmodified:

```text
https://github.com/bitnami/sealed-secrets/releases/download/v0.38.4/controller.yaml
sha256  8334764279b7dc3c758ce954c5e08cbf6d959bd977db49b872b2b722a123b202
```

It is vendored rather than pulled from a `HelmRepository` because the cluster
has no route to the internet and no chart mirror yet, and because a file that
matches the vendor's checksum byte for byte is auditable in a way a rendered
chart is not. The cost is that upgrading means fetching the new asset rather
than bumping a version field, and that cost is paid once per release.

Nothing in this file is rewritten for the airgap. The image reference stays
`docker.io/bitnami/sealed-secrets-controller:0.38.4` and the nodes resolve it
to `registry.gitlab.dev.lo` through the containerd rewrite — the same property
that let Flux itself bootstrap without `--registry`.

## Version

v0.38.4, and the choice needed care in both directions.

v0.38.2, v0.38.3 and v0.38.4 were cut within three hours of each other on
2026-07-03, and the vendor labels v0.38.3 and v0.38.4 "Incomplete release for
credentials problems". That reads as a warning to fall back to v0.38.2.

Docker Hub says otherwise: there is no `0.38.2` or `0.38.3` tag. The
credentials problem was that the image push failed, so those releases have
GitHub assets and no container image at all. v0.38.4 is the release that
finally pushed. Falling back would have produced an `ImagePullBackOff` that
looked like a broken mirror rather than an absent upstream image.

## Namespace

`kube-system`, which is the upstream default and where `kubeseal` looks
without being told otherwise. Moving it to its own namespace would mean
passing `--controller-namespace` on every seal, forever, and editing the
vendored manifest — which would cost the checksum above.

## The sealing key

The controller generates its own key pair on first start and stores it as a
Secret labelled `sealedsecrets.bitnami.com/sealed-secrets-key` in
`kube-system`. **That key is the most valuable secret in the lab**: without it
no SealedSecret in Git can be decrypted, including the OpenBao unseal keys 6b
seals. It is backed up outside the cluster — see `plan/SECRETS.md` in the
automation repository — and that backup is a risk gate, not a nicety.
